\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 And\
Takes two input parameters, P0 and P1, and returns true if they both evaluate to true. These parameters can themselves be GORP invocations \'96 think nested code like\
 X AND (Y OR Z)\
Choose\
Todo\
Compare\
Implements the same concept as the C# \'93Comparable\'94 interface \'96 returns -1, 0, or 1 depending on whether the lhs element is less than, equal to, or greater than the rhs element according to a specified mode of comparison.\
Equal\
The binary equals operator. Returns true if X and Y are equal, false otherwise.\
Exists\
Takes two input parameters, an array and a condition. Returns true if at least 1 element of the array satisfies the condition.\
Filter\
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The set filter operator. Returns all elements of an input set which satisfy a specified condition. It has two inputs (a set and a condition) and one output.\
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Forall\
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The set forall operator. It applies a specified operation to every element of a set. It has two inputs (a set and an operator) and one output.\
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 Greater\
The binary \'93greater than\'94 operator. See \'93Less\'94 for more information.\
If\
Standard \'93If\'94 conditional. If the condition is true, do the specified operation.\
Less\
The binary \'93less than\'94 operator.\
In the following example, the first parameter is itself a function, which is then checked against the constant 500 \'96 if the result of the function is less than 500, this will return true.\
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\fs16 \cf0 <Less output=" funcname.result" input="nameofinputmodel">\
	<parameters>\
		<parameter output="funcname.result" position="0">\
			<pathvariable namespace=" nameofinputmodel.subspace.etc" type="int"/>\
		</parameter>\
		<parameter position="1">500</parameter>\
	</parameters>\
</Less>\
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 MathExpression\
This is how we take MathML and turn it into running code. The input here is any valid MathML syntax (with restrictions; not all operators are supported at this time). For example:\
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\fs16 \cf0 <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">\
	<apply>\
		<plus/>\
		<ci>x</ci>\
		<cn>1</cn>\
	</apply>\
</math>\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\fs24 \cf0 This would render as \'93x + 1\'94, and does exactly that when compiled into code \'96 takes a variable named x as input and adds 1 (the \'93ci\'94 tag in MathML refers to variables, the \'93cn\'94 tag refers to constants).\
Right now, the only supported operators are:\
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sb240\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Plus\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Minus\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Times\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Divide\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Power\
{\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}Log\
\pard\pardeftab720\sb240\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0
\cf0 When representing these as XML, there can also be nested invocations of other MathExpressions. Below is a running code example that shows how a MathML XML document is converted into an executable function in C#.\
\pard\pardeftab720\pardirnatural\partightenfactor0

\f2 \cf3 //doc = <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">\cf4 \uc0\u8232 \cf3 //	        <apply>\cf4 \uc0\u8232 \cf3 //		        <plus/>\cf4 \uc0\u8232 \cf3 //		        <ci>x</ci>\cf4 \uc0\u8232 \cf3 //		        <cn>1</cn>\cf4 \uc0\u8232 \cf3 //	        </apply>\cf4 \uc0\u8232 \cf3 //      </math>\cf4 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 XmlElement? e_math = doc.SelectSingleNode(\cf5 "//Math/math"\cf4 ) \cf6 as\cf4  XmlElement;\uc0\u8232 Assert.NotNull(e_math);\u8232 \u8232 MathExpression expr = \cf6 new\cf4  MathExpression(\cf5 "MyMath"\cf4 );\uc0\u8232 \u8232 Stopwatch sw = \cf6 new\cf4  Stopwatch();\uc0\u8232 sw.Start();\u8232 expr.Compile(e_math);\u8232 sw.Stop();\u8232 Console.WriteLine(\cf5 $"Compiling MathML into C# expression tree took \{sw.ElapsedMilliseconds\}ms"\cf4 );\uc0\u8232 \u8232 SymbolicValue sv = \cf6 new\cf4  SymbolicValue \{ Base = GestaltType.g_double, Unit = \cf7 null\cf4 , Value = \cf7 183497\cf4  \}; \cf3 //This is the input value (i.e. "x")\cf4 \uc0\u8232 GorpValue gv = \cf6 new\cf4  GorpValue \{ TypeSymbol = \cf5 "double"\cf4 , Value = sv \};\uc0\u8232 GorpModelMember \cf6 value\cf4  = \cf6 new\cf4  GorpModelMember()\uc0\u8232 \{\u8232     Value = gv\u8232 \};\u8232 \cf6 value\cf4 .GestaltType = GestaltType.g_double;\uc0\u8232 \cf6 value\cf4 .Namespace = \cf5 "x"\cf4 ; \cf3 //This is how the code knows that the "x" in the MathML gets this specific value\cf4 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 GorpModel model = \cf6 new\cf4  GorpModel();\uc0\u8232 model.Members.Add(\cf6 value\cf4 .Namespace, \cf6 value\cf4 );\uc0\u8232 IModel result = expr.Invoke(model);\u8232 Double output = (Double)result.Members[\cf5 "MathExpression.Result"\cf4 ].Value.Value.Value;\uc0\u8232 \u8232 sw.Reset();\u8232 sw.Start();\u8232 \u8232 \cf6 for\cf4  (\cf6 int\cf4  i = \cf7 0\cf4 ; i < \cf7 10000\cf4 ; i++)\uc0\u8232 \{\u8232     expr.Invoke(model);\u8232 \}\u8232 \u8232 sw.Stop();\u8232 \u8232 Console.WriteLine(\cf5 $"Total ticks elapsed = \{sw.ElapsedTicks\}"\cf4 );\uc0\u8232 Console.WriteLine(\cf5 $"Average actual execution of C# expression tree took: \{((Double)sw.ElapsedTicks) / (Double)\cf7 10000\cf5 \} ticks"\cf4 );\
\
